[# Conformance gate policy — the cx-wide gate REGISTER (derived).

   DEFAULT = enforced (deny-by-default, mirroring spec/core/security.md's grant
   model): a failing fixture BLOCKS its gate. A suite marked `advisory` is the
   spec-first frontier — its failures are RUN and REPORTED but do NOT block the
   gate. Promote `advisory -> enforced` as the implementation lands; that
   promotion is the gate-progression control.

   WHERE A SUITE'S STATUS LIVES (D49a, #1633; RULED: RS-27). On the suite's own
   element: `[test-suite name=sync ring=2 gate=advisory reason='…']` (gate= is
   enforced | advisory; advisory carries a reason=). The status travels with
   the file into whatever repository carries it, and the grading core
   (vcx/corpus — the fixture shards and `cx corpus`) reads it there. The
   [module] rows below are DERIVED from those elements: a row states the
   element's gate= (an absent row or an absent element gate= is enforced) and
   an advisory row copies the element's reason= byte for byte.
   `make gates-manifest-gate` refuses a row that disagrees with its suite's
   element (naming both), an advisory element with no row, and an element
   gate= the grader refuses (scripts/gates_register_check.cx; its corpus is
   conformance/gates_register.cxd). To change a suite's status, edit its
   element and copy the change here; the element is the source.

   Resolution order for a fixture's effective gate (most specific wins):
     per-case `gate=` attr  >  the suite element's `gate=`  >  per-suite default here  >  enforced

   Per-case overrides live on the [case] itself (gate=advisory|pending|skip).
   The per-suite default= rows (code, stdlib, packages, xpath-31-parity) are
   read by the graders directly. See conformance/stdlib/AUTHORING.md and
   conformance/stdlib/SPEC-FINDINGS.md. #]

[gate-policy default=enforced

  [# core program + format suites: fully enforced (no frontier carve-out — the
     code.txt frontier is driven to zero by the spec-compliance effort). #]
  [suite name=code default=enforced]

  [# stdlib: implemented modules are enforced; not-yet-implemented modules are
     advisory (their fixtures are the spec-first worklist — red until built,
     must not block). Flip a module to enforced the moment it lands. #]
  [suite name=stdlib default=enforced
    [# implemented + enforced: bytes env format hash path random strings time uuid
       (left at the suite default; listed here for the reader) #]
    [module name=bytes   gate=enforced]
    [module name=env     gate=enforced reason='ENFORCED, all cases green; env-038 unblocked — operator-elements now propagate err-value operands uniformly with calls (§9.2), so [= [$env:vars] [$env:vars]] surfaces CXER0271 instead of comparing the two errs (SPEC-FINDINGS AK)']
    [module name=format  gate=enforced]
    [module name=hash    gate=enforced]
    [module name=path    gate=enforced]
    [module name=random  gate=enforced]
    [module name=strings gate=enforced]
    [module name=time    gate=enforced reason='full §3 impl complete + spec-faithful (ENFORCED, all cases green); §3.10 recurrence amendment landed — RFC 5545 RRULE (parse/format, BYDAY ordinals, BYSETPOS, all BY* filter/expand per §3.3.10) + 5/6-field cron + @macros, DST-aware wall-clock-local expansion, max-occurrences budget (CXER3325) and CXER3320-3326 band wired, all pure (no clock); occurrences cross-validated against a hand-rolled RFC 5545 reference for last-weekday-of-month / DST-spanning weekly / biweekly MO,WE,FR (time-117..147)']
    [module name=uuid    gate=enforced]

    [# not yet implemented -> advisory (spec-first frontier) #]
    [module name=graphql gate=enforced reason='IMPLEMENTED — the Ring 1 PURE GraphQL CLIENT codec (spec/03-approved/stdlib/graphql.md; #1429, RULED: GQL-1, 1430-c, 1430-g) lands with stdlib/graphql.cx and vcx/code/stdlib_graphql.v, so the 49 cases the corpus carried BEFORE the code are graded here. The corpus is hermetic and every one of the twelve public defs is Ring 1 and PURE — a document parsed and emitted, a request envelope built from declared variables, a response envelope read, its errors classified by extensions.code, an operation validated against a schema from SDL or from an introspection result, a document costed against a bound, and a connection page projected — so no case carries a grant=, a port, a store URL or --manual-clock, and none reaches cx-platform/http: the transport is the connector kit\'s kind=graphql adapter (connector.md §3.11) and the server half is a recorded trigger (GQL-1). graphql-001..008 are the document, 010..016 the variables and the request envelope, 020..028 the response envelope and the error classification a 200 hides, 030..036 the schema and validation, 040..047 the Relay connection the kit registers as its pagination style, and 050..059 the EIGHT ATTACK CLASSES of the spec §5. Twelve codes are allocated, CXER6500..CXER6511 inside the band governance.md §9.6 registers for this module']
    [; sync RETIRED (RULED: RS-12, RS-8, RS-27; #1591 item K3) --
       conformance/platform/sync.cxd left with the extraction of
       cx-platform-connector; the module was UNIMPLEMENTED on this tree (all
       132 cases red by construction, RULED: 1434-a) and stays so in the
       pinned repository, which grades it with `cx corpus` (RULED: RS-16) --
       the ABSENT policy is the EMPTY policy, and nothing here downgrades it.
       The row's reason text is in this file's history. ]
    [module name=soap gate=enforced reason='IMPLEMENTED — the Ring 1 PURE SOAP codec (spec/03-approved/stdlib/soap.md; #1456, RULED: 1430-c, 1430-d, 1430-g, 1456-a) lands with stdlib/soap.cx and vcx/code/stdlib_soap.v, so the 66 cases the corpus carried BEFORE the code are graded here. The corpus is hermetic and every one of the sixteen public defs is Ring 1 and PURE — an envelope built and read in ONE representation for both versions, one canonical reading of the two fault vocabularies, the WS-Addressing header, WS-Security\'s four members, the DETACHED signature over the Body and the WSDL the kit\'s ingest reads — so no case carries a grant=, a port, a store URL or --manual-clock, and none reaches cx-platform/http: the transport including TLS is the connector kit\'s kind=soap adapter (connector.md §3.12) and the server half is a landing. Time enters as an ARGUMENT and so does entropy. soap-001..014 are the FOURTEEN ATTACK CLASSES of the spec §5.1 and come first, 020..029 the envelope, 030..039 the fault, 040..045 WS-Addressing, 050..059 WS-Security, 060..067 the signature over the Body and 070..077 the WSDL. Sixteen codes are allocated, CXER6600..CXER6615 inside the band governance.md §9.6 registers for this module']
    [; connector RETIRED (RULED: RS-12, RS-8, RS-27; #1591 item K3) --
       conformance/platform/connector.cxd left whole with the extraction of
       cx-platform-connector, a pure-CX package (RS-25: code=none) --
       stdlib/connector.cx, connector.md and the two real-socket lane V test
